{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2970","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2970","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2970\",\"parent_revision\":\"89b061f86273a65e3d7d9418ae2eae9c489345a8cb2b8c46e857f73bb1404978\",\"root_revision\":\"89b061f86273a65e3d7d9418ae2eae9c489345a8cb2b8c46e857f73bb1404978\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nBacklog section names are not governed by a single canonical registry. Grooming and review agents each independently choose the section-name string they write to a backlog item, with no shared source of truth and no validation at the write boundary. This allows any agent to introduce an unregistered section name at any time, which is the underlying condition that produced issue #2956 (backlog_groom writes invisible via backlog_view) and remains open after that issue's fix lands, since nothing prevents a future agent from inventing another unregistered section name.\\n\\nThe system must provide one single canonical source of truth listing every legitimate section key and its display heading, used identically for writing and reading sections, so that: every agent that reads or writes a section resolves it through that one registry rather than an independently typed string; deprecated or historic alternate names for a section resolve to the same canonical key through a mapping kept separate from the canonical registry itself; and there is a documented, discoverable convention for how to register a new section when one is legitimately needed, so adding a section is a deliberate, tracked step rather than an implicit side effect of an agent's prompt wording.\\n\\n**User-provided context**: User specified: \\\"even if it was a single python file that had one strenum in it. that could be read by an agent to see what its section name maps to, and at the same time used in the code to do the mapping. just one. width a note in the architecture that says, when adding sections here is how you add an agent, use this template, append to this enum, or dict, or struct... If there are alias names for sections to pick up deprecated or historic names of sections, then a second map can be added that says the different strings that might be used to represent 'fact_check' for example, 'Facts check', 'Fact Checker'.\\\"\\n\\nEvidence gathered this session: only 3 of the actually-used section names (fact_check, rt_ica, issue_classification, plus the special groomed key) are registered in plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/rendering.py's SECTION_HEADING dict. Confirmed independently hardcoded, unregistered section-name string literals in plugins/development-harness/agents/fact-checker.md (section=\\\"Fact-Check\\\"), rtica-assessor.md (section=\\\"RT-ICA\\\"), impact-analyst.md (section=\\\"Impact Radius\\\", plus Files/Output-Evidence/suggested_location referenced as expected sections), and backlog-item-groomer.md (writes ## Groomed via a separate groomed_content param). The #2956 bug report additionally names Resources, Impact, Dependencies, Priority, Benefits, Research, Design Intent Alignment, Acceptance Criteria, Expected Behavior as corrupted section names, none of which are registered. backlog_groom's MCP section parameter is a plain str with no Literal/enum validation at the boundary. `swarm.md`'s groomer teammate spec is itself the direct source of most of these names \u2014 it instructs producing subsections \\\"Reproducibility, Priority, Impact, Benefits, Expected Behavior, Acceptance Criteria, Files, Resources, Dependencies, Effort\\\", none registered in SECTION_HEADING.\\n\\n**Discovery-phase requirement**: grooming this item's discovery phase must identify every place a section name is used \u2014 as a structural identifier (dict/enum key, MCP `section=` parameter value, storage key) or as prose (display heading, title text) \u2014 across all `.py` and `.md` files in the repo, not just the files already named above, to produce a holistic consolidation covering every section actually in use. The canonical registry + alias-map methodology this item designs must also apply recursively to subsections (e.g. `GroomedData.subsections`, rendered as `### ` headings under a parent `## ` section \u2014 see `rendering.py`'s `GROOMED_SUBSECTION_ORDER`) \u2014 not as a special case bolted on separately, but as the same registry/alias/append-point pattern applied one level deeper.\\n\\n**Design decision \u2014 recovery of already-corrupted items (user, 2026-08-18)**: rather than a bulk migration script rewriting `unknown__`-prefixed keys back to canonical keys across every affected GitHub issue (per #2974's audit, 357 of 358 cached items), normalize on read instead: when an item is loaded (from local cache or freshly parsed from GitHub), any `unknown__{key}` entry is resolved against the current canonical registry, and if `{key}` is now a registered canonical section, the in-memory item exposes it under `{key}` rather than `unknown__{key}`. No bulk rewrite of live GitHub issues is required \u2014 if and when an item is groomed again, the write path naturally persists it under `{key}` going forward. Items never touched again keep `unknown__{key}` in raw storage indefinitely but are read correctly every time regardless.\\n\\nImplementation must apply this at the point where the raw stored dict becomes the in-memory `BacklogItem.sections` (local YAML load, or `parse_issue_body` for a GitHub fetch) \u2014 not only at the rendering/display layer \u2014 since `sections=[...]` filtering and `sections_index` (the exact surfaces #2974 found give zero signal on a miss) operate on that in-memory dict. A display-only fix would not correct programmatic reads.\\n\\nOpen question for implementation to resolve: merge rule when an item has both a legacy `unknown__{key}` entry and an already-correctly-keyed `{key}` entry (possible once some items are re-groomed post-fix while others aren't touched). `reconciliation.py`'s existing entry-merge rules (longer content wins, struck state wins, unique entries preserved) are the candidate to reuse rather than defining a second merge policy.\\n\\n**Additional requirements (user, 2026-08-18):**\\n\\n1. **Write path must always reduce to the canonical name.** If a caller supplies an alias (a deprecated/historic name from the alias map) or a legacy `unknown__{key}` form on write, the persisted result must be the canonical key \u2014 never the alias, never `unknown__`. The alias map and the `unknown__` read-tolerance above exist only to correctly interpret *existing* content; they are not valid targets for new writes.\\n2. **Emit a diagnostic to stderr whenever a write actually falls back to `unknown__`** (i.e. the supplied section name matches neither the canonical registry nor the alias map). This makes future invented/unmapped section names visible immediately instead of silently accumulating the way the current 126+ unique `unknown__` keys did \u2014 the entire discovery problem this item solves existed because there was no signal when an agent used a name outside the registry.\\n\\n**Corruption-source hypotheses (unverified \u2014 record both, do not treat either as confirmed generally):**\\n\\n- **User's hypothesis**: not all affected issues were created through the dh plugin's own workflow. When the backlog MCP server fails to connect to GitHub, an agent may take the `gh` CLI or a generic GitHub MCP directly and freehand a issue body, bypassing the create/groom templates and canonical section names entirely \u2014 plausible explanation for verbose, prose-style one-off key names in the long tail.\\n- **Directly observed counter-evidence for one specific item, does not confirm or rule out the hypothesis generally**: issue #1774's raw GitHub body was checked directly (`gh api .../issues/1774 --jq '.body'`) and shows two separate `## Problem` headings (line 10 and line 270), a duplicated `## Acceptance Criteria`-shaped heading, and a full second wave of top-level sections (`## Story`, `## Evidence`, `## Goal`, `## Scope Boundary`, `## Related`, `## Context`, `## Design Intent Alignment`, `## Dispatch Site Audit Results`, `## Impact Radius`) following an original `## Groomed (2026-04-13)` pass and a later `## Regroom 2026-04-14 \u2014 Corrected Premise (Authoritative)` section. This item went through the dh grooming workflow more than once, and each re-groom pass appended a fresh set of top-level sections rather than merging into ones already present \u2014 a distinct mechanism from freehand issue creation, and direct evidence (for this one item) that corruption compounds across repeated re-groom passes. Only #1774 was checked this way; whether this mechanism, the freehand-creation hypothesis, or both account for the other 356 affected items is undetermined.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **maintainer of the codebase**, I want to **no canonical section-name registry \u2014 grooming agents hardcode arbitrary section names independently** so that **the code is cleaner and more maintainable**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 architecture review with user, 2026-08-18\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-18\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## Investigation Update 2026 08 20\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**Verdict: RESOLVED.** Implemented by PR #2987 (commit `e9c1d72d`, \\\"canonical section-name registry with write-boundary validation\\\"), merged to `main` and confirmed an ancestor of current HEAD.\\n\\nEvidence checked against current source:\\n- `backlog_core/section_registry.py`: `SectionKey`/`SubsectionKey` StrEnums, `SECTION_HEADING`/`SECTION_NAME_ALIASES` dicts, `resolve_section_name()`/`resolve_subsection_name()` \u2014 a leaf module with no internal imports, importable by any caller without a cycle.\\n- Used identically by the write path (`operations.py`), read path (`rendering.py`), GitHub-parse path (`github_sync.py`), and the legacy `.md` parser (`parsing.py`).\\n- Alias map (`SECTION_NAME_ALIASES`) kept as a separate dict from the canonical enum, per the original spec.\\n- Documented convention for adding new sections lives in the module docstring and `backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md`.\\n- All hardcoded `section=\\\"...\\\"` literals swept across `plugins/development-harness/agents/*.md` and `skills/**/*.md` resolve to registered `SectionKey` display headings \u2014 none orphaned.\\n- Recursive subsection mirror (`SubsectionKey`, `SUBSECTION_NAME_ALIASES`, `resolve_subsection_name()`) exists and is wired into `github_sync._parse_groomed_section`.\\n- Write path always reduces to canonical form (`operations._normalize_section_key`); unregistered names emit an unconditional stderr diagnostic (`operations._warn_unregistered_section`) and are also recorded to `Output.warnings`.\\n- Normalize-on-read healing (`rendering.normalize_unknown_sections`) wired into both YAML load paths.\\n- Covered by `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_section_registry.py` (802 lines).\\n\\nClosing as resolved \u2014 this repo's convention (`fix-delegation-discipline.md`) deliberately omits `Fixes #N` trailers, so the merge did not auto-close this issue; closing manually now with the reference.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"93e68e62ad4e226e023562628623a28fe67b371cd33e9a4ebbc20e2882c3b0d8\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPtXc8A\"}"}